/**
 * @defgroup DspCore_Processing Processing interfaces
 * @brief MIMO, block-oriented contracts for composing signal processors.
 *
 * Include `<DspCore_Processing.h>` or `<DspCore/Processing.h>`.
 *
 * A @ref DspCore::Processing::ProcessorBase receives one input block per ordered
 * input port and writes one output block per ordered output port. It supports
 * one-value blocks as the sample-by-sample case without introducing a separate
 * sample interface. It does not own those blocks: callers choose external
 * storage, while a future graph may manage its own private working storage.
 *
 * This module deliberately does not define a graph, port-format taxonomy,
 * latency policy, audio device, scheduler, plugin ABI or allocator. Those
 * policies require a concrete consumer. DSP Designer may consume this
 * interface, but it is an independent application and is not a DspCore
 * dependency.
 *
 * @section DspCore_Processing_Contracts Contracts
 *
 * - **SPEC-PROC-1**: A processor declares its current ordered input/output port
 *   counts. Calls provide exactly that many block views.
 * - **SPEC-PROC-2**: Input storage is readable and output storage is writable
 *   only for the call. Neither is retained or owned by the processor.
 * - **SPEC-PROC-3**: Each call declares its valid output extent in
 *   `OutputBlock::size`. A failed calculation may commit and report a valid
 *   prefix; each derived processor documents that behavior. Malformed ports,
 *   invalid views and insufficient output capacity are rejected before
 *   processing begins.
 * - **SPEC-PROC-4**: A processor documents its accepted block sizes, output
 *   cadence, aliasing support and intrinsic latency. The base interface makes
 *   none of these assumptions.
 * - **SPEC-PROC-5**: Reset restores documented signal state but preserves
 *   configuration, ports and caller-owned buffers.
 *
 * FIR and IIR are SISO implementations of @ref DspCore::Processing::ProcessorBase.
 * They accept a block of any length, preserve their filter state between calls
 * and produce one output value per input value without windowing latency.
 */
